1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,849
Fivefold Veil turns Twitch chat into a research team. Here is how we built it.

2
00:00:06,867 --> 00:00:10,871
We began with Signal Lab, a collaborative mystery.

3
00:00:10,871 --> 00:00:18,983
Reviewing existing chat puzzles and deduction games shaped our focus: shared evidence, without claiming to invent deduction.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:29,198
Your feedback shaped Fivefold Veil: team domains, original sigils, flexible host control, and guided participation.

5
00:00:29,198 --> 00:00:32,767
Testing then drove layout and Twitch login fixes.

6
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:39,828
The streamer names a room, chooses the group size and timer, then invites the audience.

7
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:45,332
Choose viewers lead, shared control, or host led.

8
00:00:45,332 --> 00:00:52,201
In shared control, viewers experiment and vote while the host confirms discoveries and controls the clock.

9
00:00:53,233 --> 00:00:57,207
Viewers enter a room code and display name to join a team.

10
00:00:57,207 --> 00:00:59,631
They can also simply watch.

11
00:01:00,633 --> 00:01:05,132
Choose a color, shape, and pulse count using the emoji keyboard.

12
00:01:05,132 --> 00:01:10,185
Send the experiment and inspect the guardian’s response.

13
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:14,079
The shared journal records the evidence.

14
00:01:14,079 --> 00:01:19,272
An experiment, or correctly linking existing evidence, earns a vote.

15
00:01:19,272 --> 00:01:22,276
Even a failed signal can eliminate a theory.

16
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:28,524
Confirm five discoveries, then combine their rules into one shared signal.

17
00:01:28,524 --> 00:01:33,372
That is the objective; these screenshots do not show a completed win.

18
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:37,919
Step by step guidance highlights the controls.

19
00:01:37,919 --> 00:01:42,837
Readable mode and optional reduced effects help new participants follow along.

20
00:01:43,867 --> 00:01:49,950
Six domains give teams distinct homes, original sigils, and shared landmark puzzles.

21
00:01:50,967 --> 00:01:55,440
This saved receipt shows an earlier real Twitch command reaching the game.

22
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:59,284
The surrounding gameplay screenshots come from a rehearsal.

23
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:04,879
I directed Codex to fix Twitch login disappearing after refresh.

24
00:02:04,879 --> 00:02:11,167
The work added encrypted server storage, remembered sessions, and accessible connection controls.

25
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:16,099
A production test exposed a hosting incompatibility.

26
00:02:16,099 --> 00:02:23,506
We traced it to an unsupported redirect mode and corrected it, while refusing redirects that could forward credentials.

27
00:02:24,533 --> 00:02:29,687
Regression tests cover refresh, replay protection, and disconnect races.

28
00:02:29,687 --> 00:02:37,640
These are real test results using mocked Twitch services. The full project suite passed one hundred fourteen tests.

29
00:02:38,667 --> 00:02:43,120
On the published site, the broadcaster connected successfully.

30
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:48,929
Reload restored chat without another authorization, and Disconnect removed the saved login.

31
00:02:48,929 --> 00:02:52,358
Explore apart. Think together.
